-  edit keydown. The Cypress advice names a `blur` event handler as the mover.
-- happy-dom cannot reproduce it: a single keydown path removes an attached node cleanly, and
-  happy-dom's own `removeChild` on a detached node throws the same way a real browser does, so no
-  behavioural in-suite scenario reddens. Forcing an in-harness failure would be an impossible
-  scenario, so the behaviour is pinned by a source-level `@conformance` check instead.
-- Root cause: `render` removes stale list items with `if (stale.parentNode) stale.parentNode.removeChild(stale)`.
-  A `parentNode` truthiness test is NOT a connectivity test; in a real browser the edit `blur`
-  handler re-enters `commitEdit`/`render`, so a stale node whose `parentNode` is still truthy but
-  which is no longer that parent's child reaches a throwing `removeChild`.
-- Pin: `features/edit-removal-safety.feature` carries a `@conformance` scenario plus a matching
-  source-level conformance check in `features/support/steps.js`. The earlier pin was a FALSE GREEN:
-  it treated the `if (stale.parentNode)` truthiness test as a guard. The shipped check requires a
-  REAL connectivity guard (`node.isConnected`, `parent.contains(node)`, or `node.remove()`). The
-  check is proven: it REDDENS on the broken `if (stale.parentNode) parent.removeChild(stale)` form
-  and GREENS on the hardened `if (stale.parentNode && stale.parentNode.contains(stale))` form.
-  The in-repo `js/app.js` already carries the `c92479c` hardening, so the in-suite scenario is
-  GREEN here; the acceptance (Cypress, real browser) runs against the broken served build, so the
